1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,999
ontolagni: a middleware ontology that tells an agent what data exists where.

2
00:00:04,999 --> 00:00:10,264
This is a short tour of the idea, how to use it, and what a reproducible demo measures.

3
00:00:10,264 --> 00:00:13,108
It also says plainly what is not measured here.

4
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:19,050
Imagine a store with hundreds of thousands of files from many projects and experiments.

5
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,252
An agent that guesses paths, or lists everything, wastes time and context.

6
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:27,795
And an index someone wrote by hand slowly drifts away from what is really there.

7
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:33,678
The idea is to derive the ontology from the catalog listing and nothing else.

8
00:00:33,678 --> 00:00:36,853
Each file path contributes to its ancestor groups.

9
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:42,884
Each group becomes a node with file and byte counts, formats, tags parsed from its name such as

10
00:00:42,884 --> 00:00:46,820
model, chip and date, and a digest over everything beneath it.

11
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,058
Anything that cannot be derived is simply left out.

12
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,794
Using it takes four commands.

13
00:00:52,794 --> 00:00:54,049
Build from a catalog.

14
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:59,609
Find nodes by plain words, and each result carries its counts and the next traceDB command to

15
00:00:59,609 --> 00:00:59,848
run.

16
00:00:59,848 --> 00:01:02,120
Where gives the owner chain of a path.

17
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:06,962
And verify rebuilds from the catalog and exits with code two if anything drifted.

18
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:09,537
It is built for agents.

19
00:01:09,537 --> 00:01:12,348
It is siloed, with standard library only.

20
00:01:12,348 --> 00:01:18,382
Every result carries its cost, so the next step is a traceDB plan, then a budgeted read.

21
00:01:18,382 --> 00:01:23,182
And every node has a digest, so drift is detected rather than trusted.

22
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:30,474
To measure the mechanics, the repo includes a deterministic demo that generates a synthetic

23
00:01:30,474 --> 00:01:33,241
catalog of 60,000 files in 50 campaigns.

24
00:01:33,241 --> 00:01:34,694
It becomes 903 nodes.

25
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:38,429
The catalog listing is 10.9 megabytes.

26
00:01:38,429 --> 00:01:44,627
The ontology is 0.45, about 25 times smaller, small enough to load in 10 milliseconds.

27
00:01:44,627 --> 00:01:48,014
Building it from the catalog takes 0.4 seconds.

28
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:53,230
A find query takes a median of 0.33 milliseconds, and at worst 0.35.

29
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:58,940
When we change the hash of a single file in the catalog, verify flags exactly the 4 nodes on

30
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:02,043
that file's path, from its root down to its group.

31
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,012
Honest caveats.

32
00:02:04,012 --> 00:02:10,041
The demo catalog is synthetic, so it measures mechanics, not ranking quality on a real store.

33
00:02:10,041 --> 00:02:14,774
The 100% top one hit rate is by construction, and is only a sanity check.

34
00:02:14,774 --> 00:02:20,544
The real catalog evaluation inputs are not part of this repository, so we make no ranking

35
00:02:20,544 --> 00:02:21,581
claims about it.

36
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:23,333
ontolagni.

37
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:26,347
Find with ontolagni, fetch with traceDB.

38
00:02:26,347 --> 00:02:29,963
The code and the demo benchmark are in the repo.

